Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = stylized children's picture-book illustration; painted storybook look with gentle hand-drawn texture for every character and animal.

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A fat, bunchy VELVETEEN RABBIT with spotted brown-and-white fur, pink sateen ears, thread whiskers, and a holly sprig between his paws sits atop the BOY'S stocking.

(SOUND: paper rustles, a toy engine clicks, distant Christmas voices)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

The BOY lifts the Rabbit from the stocking and hugs him. New parcels arrive; oranges roll, tissue paper flies, and the Rabbit is left on the nursery floor.

(SOUND: children laugh, parcels tear open, a clockwork mouse whirs)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For at least two hours the Boy loved him. Then, in the excitement of new presents, the Velveteen Rabbit was forgotten.

INT. NURSERY - LATE AFTERNOON

The Rabbit rests near the fender beside the SKIN HORSE, an old brown toy horse bald in patches, his seams showing and his tail nearly bare. A painted MODEL BOAT, a tin toy, and TIMOTHY, a jointed wooden lion, crowd the cupboard behind them.

(SOUND: fire crackles, tin toys clink inside the cupboard)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The mechanical toys were very superior, and pretended they were real. The only person kind to the Rabbit was the Skin Horse.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you.

The Skin Horse turns his worn glass eye toward the Rabbit.

SKIN HORSE
When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time.

(SOUND: the nursery clock ticks steadily)

SKIN HORSE
By the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out, and you get very shabby.

SKIN HORSE
But once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. Once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

NANA, brisk in a dark dress and white apron, searches the floor as the BOY waits in bed. The Rabbit lies half-hidden in the open toy cupboard.

(SOUND: a cupboard door bumps, bed springs creak)

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

Nana drags the Rabbit out by one ear and puts him into the Boy's arms.

The Boy presses the Rabbit under his chin. The night-light glows on the mantel.

(SOUND: soft whispering beneath blankets, rain taps the window)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Boy made nice tunnels for him under the bedclothes, like the burrows the real rabbits lived in.

The Boy rolls onto the Rabbit, then curls around him protectively.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And when the Boy dropped off to sleep, the Rabbit would snuggle down close under his little warm chin.

EXT. GARDEN - SPRING DUSK

The BOY, now in a light play smock, has left the Rabbit damp and earthy in a flower-bed burrow beneath raspberry canes. Dew beads on the Rabbit's thinning velvet.

(SOUND: evening birds fade, damp grass brushes against Nana's shoes)

Nana finds the Rabbit by candlelight and rubs him with the corner of her apron.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

The BOY sits upright in bed, his hands stretched toward the shabby Rabbit. Nana stands beside him, holding the candle.

(SOUND: candle flame sputters, the nursery clock ticks)

BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The Rabbit lies against the Boy's chest. His nose is rubbed pale, his tail loosens, and his boot-button eyes have lost their polish.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The nursery magic had happened to him, and he was a toy no longer.

NANA
I declare if that old Bunny hasn't got quite a knowing expression!

EXT. WOODLAND BRACKEN - SUMMER EVENING

The BOY makes a nest for the Rabbit in deep green bracken, then runs away to play among the trees. Ants travel between the Rabbit's velvet paws.

(SOUND: cicadas hum, leaves stir, the Boy's distant footsteps fade)

Two sleek WILD RABBITS creep from the bracken. Their fur is bright, their noses twitch, and their feet shift shape as they crouch and spring.

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

The Wild Rabbit hops sideways and rises on his hind legs.

WILD RABBIT
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Rabbit sits very still, his back made in one piece like a pincushion.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I can jump higher than anything!

WILD RABBIT
Then stretch them out and show me.

The Wild Rabbit dances in a circle. The Rabbit's thread whiskers tremble.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still.

The Wild Rabbit leans close, sniffs the Rabbit's worn velvet, then jumps back.

(SOUND: bracken snaps, a wood pigeon flaps overhead)

WILD RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! The Boy said so!

The BOY'S footsteps rush nearer. White tails flash as the wild rabbits vanish into the bracken.

(SOUND: rapid feet in leaves, then a sudden quiet)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

The Rabbit remains alone as white moths flutter through the lowering light.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

The BOY lies feverish beneath bedclothes, his flushed face wet with sweat. The Rabbit, now threadbare, grey-eared, and nearly shapeless, is clutched under the Boy's hot arm.

(SOUND: strained breathing, a spoon taps a glass, rain presses against the panes)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Boy was ill. Strange people came and went in the nursery, and a light burned all night.

The Rabbit inches close to the pillow while the Boy mutters in sleep.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He never stirred, for he was afraid that if they found him some one might take him away.

Days pass in the same room. The Boy sits up weakly with a picture-book while the Rabbit rests at his side.

(SOUND: pages turn, a kettle whistles faintly downstairs)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And presently the fever turned, and the Boy got better.

INT. NURSERY - SUNNY MORNING

The windows stand wide open. The recovering BOY, wrapped in a shawl, sits on the balcony beyond the open doors. The Rabbit peeps from the bedclothes.

(SOUND: spring birds sing, curtains flutter in the breeze)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Boy was going to the seaside to-morrow. The room was to be disinfected, and all the toys he had played with in bed must be burnt.

Nana spots the Rabbit.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once.

DOCTOR
Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more.

The Rabbit's button eyes face the bright window as Nana lifts him away.

(SOUND: the balcony door closes, the Boy coughs softly)

EXT. GARDEN BEHIND THE FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

The Rabbit lies in an untied sack among old picture-books and rubbish. His brown-and-white velvet is worn thin; his whiskers are gone and his faded pink nose is damp.

(SOUND: chickens rustle in their coop, wind moves the raspberry canes)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night the Boy slept in a different bedroom, with a new bunny. The little Rabbit lay behind the fowl-house, very lonely.

The Rabbit wriggles his head through the sack opening. Beyond him, the old raspberry thicket rises dark against the moonlit garden.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He thought of the fairy huts in the flower-bed, the quiet evenings in the wood, and the wonderful day when he first knew that he was Real.

A real tear rolls down his shabby velvet nose and drops into the earth.

(SOUND: one tear lands in the soil, wind falls still)

A flower thrusts upward where the tear fell: emerald leaves and a golden cup blossom. It opens.

(SOUND: petals unfold with a faint crystalline chime)

The NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY steps from the flower. She wears pearl and dew-drop garments, flowers in her hair, and carries a soft golden glow.

FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have seen your face before, but I cannot think where.

FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved.

FAIRY
When they are old and worn out, and the children don't need them any more, then I come and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

The Fairy gathers the Rabbit in her arms and kisses his damp velvet nose.

EXT. WOODLAND GLADE - MOONLIT NIGHT

The Fairy carries the Rabbit into a silvered clearing. Wild rabbits dance on velvet grass, then stop in a ring around her.

(SOUND: night insects pulse, rabbit feet patter softly on grass)

FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land.

FAIRY
He is going to live with you for ever and ever.

The Fairy sets the Rabbit down and kisses him once more.

FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

The Rabbit sits still. A blade of grass tickles his nose. He lifts a hind toe to scratch it.

(SOUND: a tiny sneeze, then a rising shimmer of music)

He stares down: soft brown fur covers him. Long whiskers brush the grass. Strong hind legs bunch beneath him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Instead of dingy velveteen he had brown fur, soft and shiny, and his ears twitched by themselves.

The Rabbit leaps, then springs sideways and whirls among the wild rabbits.

(SOUND: many rabbit feet thump joyfully, MUSIC: bright woodwind dance)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOODLAND BRACKEN - SPRING DAY

A year later, the BOY, healthy and older in the same light play clothes, plays among the trees. Two rabbits peer from the bracken.

(SOUND: warm birdsong, children’s distant laughter, leaves whispering)

One rabbit is brown all over. The other is brown with faint old spots beneath his fur, dark round eyes, and a soft nose that seems familiar.

The BOY kneels slowly, watching him.

BOY
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The Real Rabbit holds the Boy's gaze for one quiet moment, then turns toward the sunlit woods.

(SOUND: the Rabbit's feet patter away through bracken, MUSIC: gentle strings resolving)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

FADE OUT.

THE END
